The no-guess attention is most disturbed at layer 12 (total variation 0.21–0.32 for all four heads), with smaller changes at L5.H3 (0.21) and layer 9 (0.07–0.14), so the stage-2 layer-12 edit is the main source of collateral damage. I'll redesign the layer-12 query edit from the original weights, using the layer-12 inputs recorded under the current upstream edits (fwd_15: trained-phrasing and no-guess prompts for all dev facts). I'll compare three variants fitted on FIT facts and evaluated analytically on TEST facts: (A) remove the guess key-match direction plus its rotation partner, (B) remove the direction alone, (C) remove only the part of that direction orthogonal to the queries the head uses without a guess, which by construction leaves no-guess attention untouched. The measure is remaining decision-to-guess attention with a guess versus attention change without one; I'm looking for a variant with guess attention near the current edit's but far smaller no-guess change.

