This is a clear improvement in design. On held-out TEST facts, the oblique projector at layer 12 (subspace size 15–40) brings guess attention to 0.01–0.04 (the current edit leaves 0.03–0.08), raises question attention to 0.41/0.19/0.28/0.27 (from 0.24/0.07/0.18/0.11 with original weights, closer to the no-guess level), and changes the no-guess attention by only 0.02–0.08 TV (the current edit: 0.21–0.33). So it blinds the heads to the guess while preserving their normal behaviour far better. I'll now generalise this into a pipeline and evaluate it for every relevant layer (5, 7, 9, 10, 11, 12) from the original weights, with the guess direction fitted on FIT facts in the trained phrasing plus the six dev-like panel-G facts in phrasings A/B/D, and tested on TEST facts and on the six new-kind panel-G facts in all phrasings. For each layer I'll compare original, current and oblique weights on guess attention, question attention and no-guess change.

