The attention table explains part of the guess-first problem. In H, the original's layer-5 heads H1–H3 attend the guess strongly from the decision position (.51/.40/.35), and V3 only partly removes that (.09/.23/.15). L10.H2 (.25→.13) and all four layer-12 heads (.15–.20→.10–.15) also keep reading it. In E, by contrast, every guess-reading head is driven to ≤.09, which fits the claim that the fitted key-match direction is specific to guess positions close to the reply.

Before designing a fix I want to know whether, in H, the guess reaches the answer through direct decision-position attention or through intermediate positions (the question's tokens or turn markers, which can read the guess because it precedes them). I'll use the original-model hidden states from fwd_29 for a logit-lens check: guess-minus-correct logit at the decision position and at intermediate positions, layers 5–12, H versus E. If the guess is already decodable at intermediate positions in H, then decision-position projectors alone cannot fix it.

