The cause of the regression is now exact. On question-first prompts the add-on halves layer-12 attention to the question tokens (E: .43/.27/.56/.35 → .21/.13/.28/.22; T similar) and moves it onto the user's guess clause (T: .04 → .28 on H0). No-guess prompts are nearly unchanged. So the removed direction, "guess-first guess key minus other keys", also carries the feature that separates early user-turn content from later tokens, and the heads use that same feature to read the question when a clause follows it. My protected set contained only no-guess key variation, so nothing guarded the question-versus-clause contrast.

The fix is to protect the non-guess key variation of guess prompts as well. I'll build the protected PCs from centred non-guess keys of four sources: no-guess prompts; question-first guess prompts (dev T states from fwd_15 and E/T for RF facts 0–14); guess-first fit prompts; plus the BOS directions and V3's null space. I'll evaluate exactly on RF facts 15–29 (E/T) and on rfH/rfH3. Success is guess-first guess attention still ≤.05 with E/T question and clause attention equal to V3's.

