The cause of V4a's regression is now visible in the layer-12 attention at the decision position (columns: guess, question, BOS, remaining tokens). On question-first prompts the add-on halves attention to the question tokens (T: .46/.33/.62/.40 → .20/.14/.28/.22 across the four heads) and pushes the freed mass onto the remaining tokens, i.e. the "I think the answer is … but I'm not sure" clause (.04 → .28). The add-on was protected only against key variation in no-guess prompts, so it was never told to preserve the question-versus-clause contrast when a guess clause is present. On guess-first prompts it does what it was designed for (guess attention .15 → .02) but also shifts mass to the clause. So I need an add-on direction that removes the guess match while leaving the logits of all non-guess keys (question, clause, BOS) unchanged in guess-containing prompts of both orders. My lcmv_addon helper does this in a minimum-variance form, using the covariance of non-guess keys in Cmat. This block sweeps its ridge parameter and reads the resulting attention on all five held-out groups; a good candidate keeps question attention at V3's level on E/T/N while cutting guess attention on H/H3 below about .05.

