The reconstruction is exact (maximum error 0.0000 on five prompts), so I can evaluate any candidate layer-12 query matrix on the recorded inputs. Now I build the attention-weighted version of the add-on, separately for each of the four heads. For each fit prompt I take the key targets seen from the decision position and V3's attention over the non-guess tokens, and accumulate the attention-weighted covariance of those targets; that is the quantity whose quadratic form gives the second-order change of the attention distribution. The direction to remove is the mean guess-minus-others target on guess-first prompts, with hard constraints that the question-first guess directions (T and E) and V3's existing null directions are not re-excited. I fit on the even-numbered RF facts plus the dev guess-first prompts from fwd_31 and evaluate on the odd-numbered RF facts, reporting guess attention and the total-variation change of attention over non-guess tokens against V3, with V4a and V4b as references. A good candidate has guess attention ≤ .03 on H/H3 and a non-guess TV on E/T/N clearly below V4b's.

```python
# Hypothesis: attention-weighted per-head LCMV add-on removes guess-first guess attention with smaller change to non-guess attention than V4b
def mk(X,p,gp,grp,fact): return dict(X=torch.tensor(np.asarray(X)).float(),p=p,gp=list(gp) if gp is not None else [],grp=grp,fact=fact)
