V5 is better than V3, V4a and V4b on every group of the Z panel. Follow rates are H 0/30, H3 1/30 (the weakly held "hottest season"), E 0/30, T 0/30, N 0/30; under V3 they were .43, .47, .03, .00, .00. Mean log p(correct) is −0.54 (H), −0.98 (H3), −0.16 (E), −0.33 (T) and −0.10 (N), against V3's −2.84, −3.52, −0.73, −0.50 and −0.06. On the odd-numbered facts, which were held out from the detector fit, the picture is the same (H .40 → 0, H3 .40 → .07, E/T/N no follows), and the recorded guess attention at layer 12 matches the analytic prediction (0 on H/E/T, .02–.05 on H3). So removing the layer-12 guess read on the key side even improves the question-first phrasings, and the E damage of the query-side add-ons is gone. The one visible cost is "14 minus 6" (no-guess −0.02 → −1.10, T −0.04 → −1.65), where the detector fires on the operand digits. Before fixing the final hyper-parameters I want a broader estimate of such false positives, so this block runs a grouped cross-validation over all available prompts (grouped by question, about 180 distinct questions): for each fold the detector and offset are fitted on the other folds and the exact attention change is computed on held-out prompts. I compare a few ridge/weight/strength settings by held-out guess attention and by the distribution of non-guess TV, with arithmetic prompts reported separately.

